NNAAMMEE
     uunnwwiinndd..ccoonnff — validating DNS resolver configuration file

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The unwind(8) daemon is a validating DNS resolver.

     The uunnwwiinndd..ccoonnff config file is divided into the following main sections:

     _M_a_c_r_o_s
           Definitions of variables that can be used later, simplifying the
           configuration file.

     _G_l_o_b_a_l _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Global settings for unwind(8).

     Additional configuration files can be included with the iinncclluuddee keyword.

   MMaaccrrooss
     A macro is defined with a command of the form _n_a_m_e=_v_a_l_u_e.  The macro _n_a_m_e
     can contain letters, digits, and underscores and cannot be a reserved
     word (for example, ffoorrwwaarrddeerr, ppoorrtt, or DDooTT).  Within unquoted arguments,
     the string $_n_a_m_e is later expanded to _v_a_l_u_e.

     For example:

           fwd1=192.0.2.53
           fwd2=192.0.2.153
           forwarder { $fwd1 $fwd2 }

   GGlloobbaall ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     bblloocckk lliisstt _f_i_l_e [lloogg]
             A file containing domains to block, one per line.  If a domain
             from this list is queried, uunnwwiinndd answers with a return code of
             REFUSED.  With lloogg blocked queries are logged.  The list supports
             limited wildcard syntax: domains starting with . (dot) are
             treated as any subdomains on that zone.

     ffoorrwwaarrddeerr {_a_d_d_r_e_s_s [ppoorrtt _n_u_m_b_e_r] [[aauutthheennttiiccaattiioonn nnaammee _n_a_m_e] DDooTT] ...}
             A list of addresses of DNS name servers to forward queries to.
             ppoorrtt defaults to 53.  If DDooTT is specified, use DNS over TLS when
             sending queries to the server at _a_d_d_r_e_s_s.  The default ppoorrtt is
             853.  _n_a_m_e validates the certificate of the DNS over TLS server.

     pprreeffeerreennccee {_t_y_p_e _._._.}
             A list of DNS name server types to specify the order in which
             name servers are picked when measured round-trip time medians are
             equal.  Additionally, the first mentioned type gets a time bonus.
             Validating name servers are always picked over non-validating
             name servers.  DNS name server types are, in default order of
             preference:

             DDooTT             DNS over TLS name servers configured in
                             uunnwwiinndd..ccoonnff.

             ooDDooTT--ffoorrwwaarrddeerr  Name servers configured in uunnwwiinndd..ccoonnff.  uunnwwiinndd
                             tries to opportunistically use DNS over TLS.

             ffoorrwwaarrddeerr       Name servers configured in uunnwwiinndd..ccoonnff.

             rreeccuurrssoorr        uunnwwiinndd itself recursively resolves names.

             ooDDooTT--aauuttooccoonnff   Name servers learned from autoconfiguration.  A
                             list of sources for proposals learned through au‐
                             toconfiguration is documented in resolvd(8).
                             uunnwwiinndd tries to opportunistically use DNS over
                             TLS.

             aauuttooccoonnff        Name servers learned from autoconfiguration.

             ssttuubb            Name servers learned from autoconfiguration,
                             queried using the libc functions (see
                             asr_run(3)).  Will never validate.  Useful when
                             running behind broken middle boxes that do not
                             like edns0.  DNS answers from stub name servers
                             are not cached.

     ffoorrccee [aacccceepptt bboogguuss] _t_y_p_e {_n_a_m_e _._._.}
             Force resolving of _n_a_m_e and its subdomains by the given resolver
             _t_y_p_e.  The _t_y_p_e must be listed in the pprreeffeerreennccee list to be used.
             If aacccceepptt bboogguuss is specified, validation is not enforced.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/unwind.conf  The default unwind(8) configuration file.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Block requests for domains in _/_e_t_c_/_b_l_o_c_k_l_i_s_t and log each blocked re‐
     quest:

           block list "/etc/blocklist" log

     Define a DNS over TLS (DoT) forwarder and make it the preferred resolver:

           forwarder { 192.168.1.250 port 8080 authentication name "resolver.local" DoT }
           preference { DoT }

     Where a domain requires a specific nameserver and it may only exist in a
     nameserver available on the local network, force unwind(8) to use a spe‐
     cific resolver type:

           force autoconf { domain.local }

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     rc.conf.local(8), unwind(8), unwindctl(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The uunnwwiinndd..ccoonnff file format first appeared in OpenBSD 6.5.

